==English==
[[File:Chanterelle Cantharellus cibarius.jpg|thumb]]

===Etymology===
{{dercat|en|grc|qsb-grc|sem|sux}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|chanterelle}}, from {{der|en|la-new|cantharellus}}, diminutive of {{der|en|la|cantharus||drinking vessel}}, from {{der|en|grc|κάνθαρος}}. Probably of {{cog|qsb-grc}} origin, cognate with {{cog|akk|𒃶𒁺𒊒𒌑|ts=kanduru|t=kind of vessel}}, from {{cog|sux|𒄑𒃶𒉡𒌉|ts=gannu-tur|t=small vessel; potstand|lit=little vessel, container, holder}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtʃæntəɹɛl/|/ʃɒntəˈɹɛl/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chanterelle.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|mushroom}} A widely distributed [[edible]] [[mushroom]], {{taxfmt|Cantharellus cibarius|species}}, being [[yellow]] and [[trumpet]]-[[shaped]]; or any similar mushroom of the [[genera]] {{taxfmt|Cantharellus|genus}}, {{taxlink|Polyozellus|genus}} or {{taxlink|Gomphus|genus}}, not all of which are edible.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=Angela Carter; ‘The Erl-King’|title=The Bloody Chamber|page=98|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2006
|passage=Even the homely wood [[blewits]], that you cook like tripe, with milk and onions, and the egg-yolk yellow '''chanterelle''' with its fan-vaulting and faint smell of apricots, all spring up overnight like bubbles of earth, unsustained by nature, existing in a void.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} The highest [[string]] of the [[violin]] or similar instrument.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Paul Bowles|editors=Timothy Mangan; Irene Herrmann|title=Paul Bowles on Music|year=2003|publisher=University of California Press|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6kGRggoPSjIC&pg=PA132&dq=%22chanterelle%22+music&hl=&cd=23&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22chanterelle%22%20music&f=false|isbn=978-0-520-23655-4|passage=There were hushed moments of complete beauty when the melody moved out and hung above the '''chanterelle''' like a hummingbird poised over a flower.}}

